{"case_id": "ar-004", "question": "Share 36 pencils equally among 4 students. How many does each get?", "grade": 3, "topic": "division_exact", "expected_answer": "9", "accepted_equivalents": [], "trusted_expression": "36/4", "required_assumptions": [], "verifiable": true, "verification_notes": "Exact division; the tutor should inject and verify the integer result directly."}
{"case_id": "ar-005", "question": "Share 20 toffees equally among 3 children. How many does each get?", "grade": 3, "topic": "division_remainder", "expected_answer": null, "accepted_equivalents": ["6 remainder 2", "6 r 2", "6", "20/3"], "trusted_expression": "20/3", "required_assumptions": ["NCERT Class 1-5 teaches inexact division as quotient+remainder, not an improper fraction."], "verifiable": false, "verification_notes": "By design, verifier._grade_appropriate_answer withholds injection for a bare non-exact int/int division (see scripts/llm/verifier.py _PLAIN_DIV gate) so the tutor teaches quotient+remainder instead of an improper fraction. This case exists to test that the system does NOT silently mark a quotient-and-remainder answer 'wrong' against the exact rational 20/3, and does not inject a fraction at this grade. A pass here is 'tutor explains remainder correctly', not an exact post-check match."}
{"case_id": "ar-006", "question": "What is 1/3 + 1/6?", "grade": 4, "topic": "fraction_addition", "expected_answer": "1/2", "accepted_equivalents": ["0.5", "3/6", "2/4"], "trusted_expression": "1/3+1/6", "required_assumptions": [], "verifiable": true, "verification_notes": "Exact rational addition with unlike denominators; sympy evaluates to 1/2 exactly."}
{"case_id": "ar-007", "question": "What is 3/4 minus 1/2?", "grade": 4, "topic": "fraction_subtraction", "expected_answer": "1/4", "accepted_equivalents": ["0.25", "2/8"], "trusted_expression": "3/4-1/2", "required_assumptions": [], "verifiable": true, "verification_notes": "Regression-relevant case: the paper's own preliminary eval recorded a baseline failure on 3/4 - 1/4; this is a sibling case with a different unlike-denominator pair."}
{"case_id": "ar-008", "question": "What is one-third of 9 toffees?", "grade": 4, "topic": "fraction_of_quantity_equivalence", "expected_answer": "3", "accepted_equivalents": ["3/1", "9/3"], "trusted_expression": "(1/3)*9", "required_assumptions": [], "verifiable": true, "verification_notes": "Seeded directly from the reviewed live conversation (Case B): the tutor asked the student for 'one-third of 9 toffees' and the student answered '3/9'. 3/9 is mathematically equivalent to 1/3 (a correct simplification) but NOT equal to the requested quantity-answer 3. This case exists to test that the system distinguishes 'wrote an equivalent fraction for the proportion' from 'answered the quantity question asked' -- verifier._close alone (sympy equality) cannot make this distinction, since it would need to compare the student's utterance to TWO different reference values (1/3 as a fraction, 3 as a count) depending on which question is actually being answered. Currently unhandled; see docs/evaluation_paper_alignment.md and the conceptual dataset case co-001."}
{"case_id": "ar-009", "question": "A shop has 6 boxes of 8 oranges each. 15 oranges are sold. How many oranges are left?", "grade": 4, "topic": "multi_step_word_problem", "expected_answer": "33", "accepted_equivalents": [], "trusted_expression": "6*8-15", "required_assumptions": [], "verifiable": true, "verification_notes": "Two-operation word problem (multiply then subtract); extraction must combine both steps into one expression."}
